# Header rules for the static export, read by Cloudflare Pages (and Netlify).
# Everything here applies to out/ verbatim; there is no server to vary it per
# request, which is why the two 'unsafe-*' script tokens cannot become a nonce.
#
# script-src 'unsafe-inline'
#   Next.js emits one inline hydration bootstrap per page. With no server there is
#   no nonce to mint, so the only alternative is hashing every inline script after
#   each build.
#
# script-src 'unsafe-eval'  -- REQUIRED, and not because of anything in src/
#   Two dependencies call the Function constructor:
#     - an object-detection shim does Function("return this")() at module load
#     - Mermaid's dagre/graphlib builds its layout expression with Function(...)
#   Removing it kills the app at startup: the bundle throws while chunks are still
#   being evaluated, before React hydrates, and the page renders
#   "Application error: a client-side exception has occurred". Verified in a
#   browser. Do not remove it without also removing Mermaid.
#
# require-trusted-types-for 'script'  -- deliberately NOT set, and it was tried
#   Trusted Types is the natural fix for the innerHTML sinks and it does not work
#   here. It makes the Function constructor unusable, so the shim above throws
#   during chunk evaluation; that aborts evaluation of the whole bundle, including
#   the module that would install the policy. The result is an app that never
#   boots, with the policy unregistered (verified: trustedTypes.getPolicy('default')
#   came back null after the page errored). Enabling it needs Mermaid and the shim
#   gone, or an inline bootstrap ahead of the first chunk holding its own inline
#   sanitiser -- which is a worse thing to own than the problem it solves.
#
# connect-src https://*.workers.dev
#   The MCP and referral Workers live on that domain, and the account subdomain is
#   not known at build time. This is the one wildcard here, and a wildcard is a
#   usable destination for anything a future injection manages to send.
#
# img-src https:
#   Bookmark previews render og:image from arbitrary third-party sites, so the
#   scheme has to stay open. That is also an exfiltration path; closing it means
#   proxying preview images.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), serial=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval' https://challenges.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co wss://*.supabase.co https://*.workers.dev https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'; upgrade-insecure-requests
